  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "update_errors_warnings",
    "file": "php-src/ext/date/php_date.c",
    "description": "`update_errors_warnings()` transfers `*last_errors` into the request-global `DATEG(last_errors)` when the timelib container has warnings or errors (line 2377), but then unconditionally destroys that same container (line 2380) and only clears the caller's pointer. This leaves `DATEG(last_errors)` dangling. During date module post-deactivation, `timelib_error_container_dtor(DATEG(last_errors))` reads and frees the already-freed container, producing the heap-use-after-free/double-destruction observed in `timelib_error_container_dtor()` (`ext/date/lib/timelib.c:159`). The retained error container must not be destroyed after ownership is assigned to `DATEG(last_errors)`; only an empty/unretained container should be freed.",
    "conditions": "The harness input must execute a date parsing operation that causes timelib to return a non-NULL error container with `warning_count > 0` or `error_count > 0`. The PoC PHP source `<?new DateTime('\\xff');` passes the invalid byte in the constructor string through `DateTime::__construct()` -> `php_date_initialize()` -> `timelib_strtotime()`, yielding parser diagnostics. `update_errors_warnings()` stores then frees this container, and normal request shutdown reaches the date post-deactivate callback, which dereferences the dangling global and crashes."
  },
